  "worked_solution": "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-\\sqrt{10}$ and $b=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2-\\sqrt{10}X] & ={(-\\sqrt{10})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=0.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot f(-1)+{0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+0\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 0.3+4\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =1.2-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2-\\sqrt{10}X] & =10\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =10\\cdot 1.2 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
